"04_integer_bits.mst — Dolphin's Integer BIT-MASK protocol (DolphinDart DD9).

 Every Windows flag word in the corpus is read and written through these seven
 selectors, and the world had none of them. Corpus counts (dsfork, all `.cls`):
 `allMask:` 505, `mask:set:` 343, `anyMask:` 218, `bitInvert` 71,
 `maskSet:` 67, `maskClear:` 66, `noMask:` 63 — a little over 1,300 sends.

 Found by instantiating a translated view: `View>>initialize` ends at
 `self isManaged: true`, which is `flags := flags mask: ManagedMask set: true`,
 and it answered `Class 'int' has no instance method 'mask_set_'`.

 Each body is Dolphin's own, from Core.Integer.cls — these are one-liners whose
 exact bit semantics matter, so they are transcribed rather than reinvented.
 The two divergences are deliberate and equivalent on integers: Dolphin writes
 `~~ 0` / `== 0` (identity) where this file writes `~= 0` / `= 0`, because a
 SmallInteger comparison is a value comparison in both dialects and `=` is what
 this world defines on Integer.

 `bitAnd:` / `bitOr:` are not defined here: the front-end already maps them to
 Dart's `&` and `|` (DartSelector). `bitInvert` had no mapping and no method,
 so `maskClear:` would have failed on it."
Number subclass: Integer [
    "^-1 - self — two's complement, and correct for LargeIntegers too, which a
     fixed-width `~` would not be."
    bitInvert [ ^-1 - self ]

    allMask: mask [ ^(self bitAnd: mask) = mask ]
    anyMask: mask [ ^(self bitAnd: mask) ~= 0 ]
    noMask: mask [ ^(self bitAnd: mask) = 0 ]

    maskSet: anInteger [ ^self bitOr: anInteger ]
    maskClear: anInteger [ ^self bitAnd: anInteger bitInvert ]

    "The workhorse: 343 sites. Answers a NEW integer — Dolphin's flag words are
     values, and every caller assigns the result back."
    mask: integerMask set: aBoolean [
        ^aBoolean
            ifTrue: [ self bitOr: integerMask ]
            ifFalse: [ self bitAnd: integerMask bitInvert ]
    ]
]

"── word accessors, Dolphin's own ───────────────────────────────────────────

 `View>>wmCommand:wParam:lParam:` splits WM_COMMAND's wParam with these: the
 notification code is the high word and the control id is the low word. Both
 are verbatim from `Core.Integer`.

 `highWord` masks after shifting rather than merely shifting, which matters
 here and not in Dolphin: a Dolphin SmallInteger is 30-bit and wParam arrives
 already truncated, whereas this port's integers are full 64-bit Dart ints, so
 an unmasked shift would carry the upper half of a 64-bit wParam into the
 answer. Keeping Dolphin's mask makes the two agree."
Object subclass: Integer [
    lowWord [ ^self bitAnd: 16rFFFF ]
    highWord [ ^(self bitShift: -16) bitAnd: 16rFFFF ]
    lowSWord [
        "The low word as a SIGNED 16-bit value — coordinates in lParam are
         signed, and a window dragged above or left of the origin gives
         negative ones."
        | w |
        w := self bitAnd: 16rFFFF.
        ^w > 16r7FFF ifTrue: [ w - 16r10000 ] ifFalse: [ w ]
    ]
    highSWord [
        | w |
        w := (self bitShift: -16) bitAnd: 16rFFFF.
        ^w > 16r7FFF ifTrue: [ w - 16r10000 ] ifFalse: [ w ]
    ]

    "── lowPart / highPart: 16 BITS HERE, AND THAT IS A DECISION ─────────
     Dolphin spells these in HALF-POINTER width:

         lowPart        ^self bitAnd: VMConstants.HalfPtrMask
         lowPartSigned  | part | part := self lowPart.
                        ^part > VMConstants.HalfPtrMax
                            ifTrue: [(part bitXor: VMConstants.HalfPtrMask) bitInvert]
                            ifFalse: [part]
         highPartSigned ^(self bitShift: ##(VMConstants.HalfPtrBits negated)) lowPartSigned

     On its 32-bit build half-a-pointer IS 16 bits, so these ARE LOWORD and
     HIWORD, and every use of them in this corpus is Win32 message cracking:
     `wmSetCursor:` indexes a hit-test table with `lParam lowPartSigned + 2`
     (HTERROR is -2), `WindowsEvent>>x`/`>>y` take the mouse position out of
     lParam, `ShellView` tests `lParam lowPartSigned == -2`.

     Rule 1 says re-derive the width rather than carry the literal over — and
     re-deriving it gives 16, not 32. WM_ message parameters are packed with
     MAKELPARAM/LOWORD/HIWORD, which are 16-bit macros on x64 exactly as on
     Win32; the pointer got wider but the packing did not. Widening these to
     half of 64 would read a mouse x of 100 and a y of 200 as the single
     number 13107300, and the hit-test lookup would run off the end of its
     table for every value.

     So: the same 16 bits Dolphin gets, reached deliberately instead of by
     inheritance. `lowSWord`/`highSWord` above already are these; the corpus
     asks for them under Dolphin's names, so those names answer."
    lowPart [ ^self lowWord ]
    highPart [ ^self highWord ]
    lowPartSigned [ ^self lowSWord ]
    highPartSigned [ ^self highSWord ]

    "`asUIntPtr` is the one that genuinely IS pointer-width: Dolphin masks
     with VMConstants.IntPtrMask to force a value into an unsigned machine
     word before handing it to an API. Here that mask is 64 bits wide.
     `DialogView>>endDialog:` sends it to a plain 0."
    asUIntPtr [ ^self bitAnd: 16rFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF ]
]
